  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math>&nbsp;is giving effervescence with sodium bicarbonate, it means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math>&nbsp;contain carboxylic acid functional group.&nbsp;The compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>&nbsp;is reacting with Hinsberg&#39;s reagent and giving solid which soluble in sodium hydroxide means&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>&nbsp;contain primary amine functional group. Hence, The compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></math>can be a secondary amide. The overall reaction is shown below.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/4da61d8b-93ad-41a2-809d-f1e74ff24b95-image.png\" style=\"width: 600px; height: 165px;\" /></p></blockquote>",
